course: chinese-street
lesson: 14
title: Games: rock-paper-scissors, hide and seek, cheat!
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Who goes first? «shítou jiǎndāo bù!» — stone, scissors, cloth — shouted on the throw. Cloth (bù) beats stone, stone beats scissors, scissors beat cloth, the same as rock-paper-scissors.
explain: Many games are ones you already know under other names: zhuō mícáng is hide and seek; «yī èr sān mùtourén!» (one, two, three, wooden man!) is Grandma's footsteps; tiào shéng is skipping. And lǎoyīng zhuō xiǎojī (eagle catches the chicks) has a hen guarding a wobbly line of chicks from the eagle — play it on grass, because the wobbly line often tumbles over.
explain: The game ends with «wǒ yíng le!» (I won!) and «nǐ shū le!» (you lost!), or a píngjú (draw) and «zài lái yí cì!» (again!).
explain: Then the trouble: «nǐ zuòbì!» (you're cheating!) and the sharper «bié shuǎlài!» (stop being a sore loser!), until someone stomps off: «bù wánr le!» — and five minutes later says «wǒ yě yào wánr».
fact: shítou jiǎndāo bù («SHR?-toe JYEN-dow BOO») is rock-paper-scissors, literally stone, scissors, cloth; cloth beats stone.
fact: zhuō mícáng («JWAW MEE?-tsahng?») is hide and seek; «it» counts to ten in Chinese and shouts wǒ lái le! (here I come!).
fact: yī èr sān mùtourén («ee AR SAHN MOO-toe-run?») is the freeze game like Grandma's footsteps; move after «mùtourén» and you're out.
fact: lǎoyīng zhuō xiǎojī («LAOW-ying JWAW SHYOW-jee») is eagle catches the chicks, a chain game where the hen protects the chicks behind her.
fact: tiào shéng («TYOW SHUNG?») is skipping with a rope; tiào (jump) falls, while high tiāo means to pick; tiào píjīn is elastics.
fact: wǒ yíng le («waw YING? luh») means I won; shouted too much, people call you déyì (smug).
fact: nǐ shū le («nee SHOO luh») means you lost; shū (lose) sounds just like shū (book), so some families avoid giving books at New Year.
fact: zuòbì («DZWAW-BEE») means cheating, a heavy word for tests, but just a playground shout in tag.
fact: shuǎlài («SHWIGH-LIGH») means refusing to accept the rules, being a sore loser; it stings more than zuòbì, so use it only when it's true.
fact: bù wánr le («BOO WAR luh») means I'm not playing any more, the usual end of a quarrel.
fact: zài lái yí cì («DZYE LIGH? ee TSZ») means one more time; zài means again, and zài lái alone works too.
fact: píngjú («PING?-JOO?») is a draw, «an even game», since píng means flat or even.
exercise: What is shítou jiǎndāo bù? | Rock-paper-scissors | Hide and seek | Skipping | Tag
exercise: In shítou jiǎndāo bù, what beats stone? | Cloth (bù) | Scissors (jiǎndāo) | Another stone | Nothing beats stone
exercise: What does «it» shout in zhuō mícáng after counting? | wǒ lái le! | zhuā zhù le! | píngjú! | bù wánr le!
exercise: What happens in yī èr sān mùtourén if you move after «mùtourén»? | You're out | You win the game | You become the wooden man | You get a second go
exercise: Which British game is yī èr sān mùtourén like? | Grandma's footsteps | Hopscotch on the pavement | Musical chairs at a party | Conkers in the autumn
exercise: In lǎoyīng zhuō xiǎojī, who protects the chicks? | The hen | The eagle | The farmer | The fox
exercise: Why play lǎoyīng zhuō xiǎojī on grass? | The wobbly line often tumbles over | Eagles live on grass | It's too slow on concrete | It's a rule from the emperor
exercise: tiào shéng is | Skipping with a rope | Jumping on a trampoline | Picking teams | Hopscotch
exercise: How do you say I won? | wǒ yíng le | nǐ shū le | píngjú | zài lái
exercise: Why might a granny avoid giving books at New Year? | shū (book) sounds like shū (lose) | Books are far too expensive at New Year | Kids should play, not read, on holidays | Books bring rain
exercise: What does nǐ zuòbì mean? | You're cheating! | You're winning again! | You're far too slow! | You're it now, run!
exercise: What is shuǎlài? | Refusing the rules, being a sore loser | Winning fairly, by the rules of the game | Counting to ten | Hiding well where no one can find you
exercise: Which stings more? | shuǎlài | zuòbì | píngjú | zài lái
exercise: A friend stomps off: «bù wánr le!». What did he say? | I'm not playing any more! | I want to play with you too! | One more time, please! | I won!
exercise: What does zài lái yí cì mean? | One more time! | Game over, time to go! | You lost, bad luck! | Not fair, that's cheating!
exercise: The football ends 2–2. That is a | píngjú | yíng | shū | zuòbì
exercise: Why is a draw called píngjú? | píng means flat or even | píng means bored, nobody won | píng means apple | píng means cheat, so nobody counts
exercise: What do people call you if you shout wǒ yíng le too much? | déyì, smug | píngjú, a draw | shuǎlài, a sore loser | zuòbì, a cheater
test: Ming throws cloth, you throw scissors. Who wins? | You: scissors cut cloth | Ming: cloth wraps scissors | Nobody, píngjú | Ming, because he's older
test: «ming moved after mùtourén but says he didn't, he's shuǎlài-ing» If he really moved, is shuǎlài fair? | Yes, refusing to accept being caught is shuǎlài | No, shuǎlài means winning the game fair and square | No, shuǎlài is a hello | Yes, but only at school
test: You lose and shout «nǐ zuòbì!» though nobody cheated. What's the problem? | Accusing cheating just because you lost isn't fair | Nothing, it's polite and everyone says it after losing | zuòbì means «well done» | It means you won the game after all
test: What would happen if a kid shouted «bù wánr le!» in a sulk? | Often, five minutes later, «wǒ yě yào wánr» | The game must end for ever and ever, those are the rules | He wins the game, that's the rule | Everyone must go home
test: In a cartoon the eagle protects the chicks from the hen. What's wrong? | The hen protects the chicks; the eagle catches | Nothing, that's exactly how the game is played | There are no chicks in it, only eagles | The chicks catch the eagle
test: «tiāo shéng?» you ask, high tone. What did you say? | «pick rope», not skip; skipping is tiào shéng | Skipping, exactly right, with the right tone | Rope soup | Hide and seek, the high-tone version
test: Rock-paper-scissors ends with both of you throwing stone. What does Ming say? | píngjú, zài lái! | wǒ yíng le! | nǐ shū le! | nǐ zuòbì!
test: Your cousin gives Ming's granny a book at New Year. Why might she smile only politely? | shū (book) sounds like shū (lose) | Grannies all hate reading books | Books are always rude gifts | The book was red, the funeral colour
test: «wǒ yíng le wǒ yíng le wǒ yíng le!!!» says Ming for ten minutes. What might friends call him? | déyì, smug | píngjú | lǎoyīng | mùtourén
test: Two kids argue whether stone or cloth wins. Who is right? | Cloth wins, it wraps the stone | Stone wins, it tears the cloth | Neither, it's a draw every time | Whoever is older
test: Everyone loved the game. What do you shout? | zài lái yí cì! | bù wánr le! | nǐ shū le! | bié shuǎlài!
exam: Identify the outcome when cloth (bù) is opposed to stone (shítou) in the game shítou jiǎndāo bù. | Cloth prevails | Stone prevails | The result is invariably a draw | The game is declared void
exam: Ascertain the reason for which certain families refrain from presenting books at the New Year. | The word for book is homophonous with the word for losing | Books are considered unsuitable for children at festivals | Reading is prohibited during festivals | Books are associated with funerals and mourning
exam: Select the response consistent with the established distinction between zuòbì and shuǎlài. | The former denotes cheating, the latter refusing to accept defeat | Both denote victory | The former denotes a draw, whereas the latter denotes a rematch | Both denote concealment in hide and seek
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the game yī èr sān mùtourén? | Players freeze when the counter turns; any who move are out | Players hide while one seeks them | Players skip a rope in turns, counting aloud to one hundred | A hen protects chicks from an eagle
